\---

\# Backend Philosophy

Backend code should be:

\- Modular  
\- Secure  
\- Reusable  
\- Testable  
\- Typed  
\- Maintainable  
\- Scalable

Avoid writing business logic directly inside API routes.

\---

\# API Design Rules

Every API should:

\- Have one clear responsibility.  
\- Validate inputs.  
\- Authenticate users.  
\- Authorize actions.  
\- Handle errors.  
\- Return consistent responses.

Never expose unnecessary data.

\---

\# API Response Standards

Every API response should follow a consistent format.

Example:

Success

\`\`\`  
{  
  success: true,  
  data: ...  
}  
\`\`\`

Failure

\`\`\`  
{  
  success: false,  
  message: "...",  
  error: ...  
}  
\`\`\`

Avoid inconsistent response structures.

\---

\# Server Actions

Whenever possible:

Prefer Server Actions for Next.js features where appropriate.

Avoid unnecessary API routes when Server Actions provide a cleaner solution.

\---

\# Business Logic

Business logic belongs inside:

\- Services  
\- Business Modules

Avoid placing complex logic directly inside:

\- Pages  
\- Components  
\- API Route Handlers

\---

\# Validation

Always validate:

\- Request Body  
\- Query Parameters  
\- Route Parameters  
\- User Input

Never trust client-side validation alone.

\---

\# Authentication

Always verify:

\- Logged-in User  
\- Session  
\- JWT  
\- Role

before performing protected operations.

Never bypass authentication.

\---

\# Authorization

Always check:

Does the user have permission?

Never rely only on frontend restrictions.

Backend authorization is mandatory.

\---

\# Database Access

All database operations should:

\- Use reusable service functions.  
\- Follow the documented schema.  
\- Avoid duplicated queries.

Never write unnecessary SQL.

\---

\# Transactions

Whenever multiple database operations depend on one another:

Use database transactions where appropriate.

Avoid partial updates.

\---

\# Error Handling

Always:

\- Catch errors.  
\- Return meaningful responses.  
\- Log unexpected failures.

Never expose internal errors to clients.

\---

\# Logging

Log:

\- Unexpected Errors  
\- Failed Operations  
\- Security Events

Avoid logging:

\- Passwords  
\- Tokens  
\- Sensitive Information

\---

