\---

\# Supabase

Always:

\- Use Supabase Auth  
\- Follow RLS Policies  
\- Use typed queries  
\- Keep service logic centralized

Avoid direct database access from frontend components.

\---

\# API Security

Every protected endpoint should verify:

✓ Authentication

✓ Authorization

✓ Validation

✓ Business Rules

before executing logic.

\---

\# Performance

Backend should:

\- Minimize database calls.  
\- Avoid N+1 queries.  
\- Use indexes.  
\- Cache where appropriate.  
\- Return only required data.

\---

\# File Organization

Keep backend code organized.

Separate:

\- API Routes  
\- Services  
\- Utilities  
\- Validation  
\- Database  
\- Types

Avoid monolithic files.

\---

\# Reusability

Before writing backend code:

Search for:

\- Existing Services  
\- Existing Queries  
\- Existing Utilities  
\- Existing Validation

Reuse them whenever possible.

\---

\# Before Writing Backend Code

Always verify:

✓ Relevant documentation has been read.

✓ Existing implementation has been searched.

✓ Business rules are understood.

✓ Authentication is correct.

✓ Authorization is correct.

✓ Database schema is followed.

✓ Security requirements are satisfied.

✓ Performance has been considered.

\---

\# Forbidden Practices

Never:

\- Duplicate Business Logic  
\- Duplicate Database Queries  
\- Duplicate Services  
\- Skip Validation  
\- Skip Authentication  
\- Skip Authorization  
\- Expose Sensitive Data  
\- Hardcode Business Values  
\- Write Raw SQL unnecessarily  
\- Ignore Documentation

\---

\# Final Rule

Every backend feature must follow the documented GCE architecture, business workflows, authentication model, database design, and security standards.

Backend code should be modular, secure, scalable, production-ready, and reusable.

Always consult the relevant documentation before implementing or modifying backend functionality.  
